font-weight:700">Good morning, world. </span><span>Three years ago, Israel =
suffered the worst attack in its history. On Oct. 7, 2023, Hamas-led gunmen=
 killed nearly 1,200 people and took 251 people hostage. Some called it a p=
ogrom. It shattered any sense of security Israelis had.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>W=
hat followed has radically reshaped the region: Israel=E2=80=99s war agains=
t Hamas in Gaza, which has killed more than 75,000 Palestinians and prompte=
d allegations of genocide that Israel has denied. Military campaigns in Leb=
anon and Iran.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;co=
lor:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But inside Israel, the trauma of Oct. 7 its=
elf is still fresh. Many Israelis feel there has been no reckoning with the=
 failures that preceded the attack at home. When they go to the polls in tw=
o weeks, for the first time since the attacks, one thing many will be votin=
g on is who they think is most likely to keep them safe. I spoke to my coll=
eague Isabel Kershner about what many describe as the most consequential el=
ections in the country=E2=80=99s history.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal=
 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Also:</span></p>=
