tenham,georgia,serif;margin:0 0 10px"><span>=E2=80=98What kind of a country=
 do you want Israel to be=3F=E2=80=99</span></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weigh=
t:700">Isabel, it=E2=80=99s been three years since the Oct. 7 attacks. How =
have these attacks changed Israel=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Profoundly. Israel is=
 no longer holding back. It=E2=80=99s going beyond its borders and occupyin=
g territory. It=E2=80=99s rejecting any international condemnation of its c=
onduct in Gaza and the West Bank. You see a lot of Israelis who are forever=
 traumatized by that one day. And that manifests in two ways: There=E2=80=
=99s a very large consensus within Israel in support of the military campai=
gns in Gaza, in Iran and against Hezbollah in Lebanon.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>A=
t the same time, people have lost trust in everything they previously assum=
ed would keep them safe at home. Hamas and its allies carried out the attac=
k. But leading up to the attack, there were years of government failure: fa=
iled policies in Gaza, failure to gather intelligence and failure to interp=
ret available intelligence. And on the day itself, the military was slow to=
 respond. All that has shaken people=E2=80=99s confidence in the state.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">What does that mean for the electio=
ns coming up in two weeks=E2=80=99 time=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In one sense, i=
f you look at the polls, the country is divided along very familiar lines, =
between those who broadly support Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and tho=
se who broadly oppose him. But the stakes feel a lot higher. Many people ar=
e saying this is the most important election in Israel=E2=80=99s history. T=
he question hanging over this campaign is: What kind of a country do you wa=
nt Israel to be=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">How so=3F</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>The current government is the most right-wing and religiously=
 conservative that Israel has ever had. This government is still pursuing a=
 version of the judicial reform that critics say would compromise the indep=
endence of the judiciary and which led to mass protests before Oct. 7. And =
this government is enabling a huge expansion of settlements in the occupied=
 West Bank and has taken little action against violent extremist settlers t=
here.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;=
margin:0 0 15px"><span>So looking ahead, people are having to decide: Do th=
ey want more of that=3F Or do they want to go back to a more secular libera=
l democracy with different priorities=3F</span></p><table border=3D"0" cell=
padding=3D"0" cellspacing=3D"0" style=3D"width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style=
=3D"text-align:center"><img width=3D"500" height=3D"625" style=3D"display:i=
