</table><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0=
 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Who are the main personalities and=
 parties to watch=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>On Netanyahu=E2=80=99s side, there ar=
e the far-right parties and the ultra-Orthodox parties. Then you have the o=
pposition, which is fragmented. The front-runner is a center-right former g=
eneral named Gadi Eisenkot, who leads the Yashar party, but overall, it=E2=
=80=99s a very mixed bag. There are right-wing parties; there are more cent=
rist parties; the traditional Israeli left in favor of a two-state solution=
 barely exists anymore. There are also some Arab parties. What unites most =
of the opposition is that they are against what they see as the zealotry of=
 the current government and they want it to take responsibility for the fai=
lure to prevent Oct. 7. They want an independent inquiry into Oct. 7, which=
 Netanyahu has so far blocked.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px =
georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">W=
hat are Netanyahu=E2=80=99s chances of winning another term=3F</span></p><p=
 style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><=
span>Many of the polls are telling us Netanyahu=E2=80=99s bloc and the oppo=
sition are running neck and neck. The predominantly Jewish parties in the o=
pposition could win a majority together with the Arab parties. But many Isr=
aeli voters, even those who don=E2=80=99t like Netanyahu, don=E2=80=99t wan=
t their government to rely on Arab parties.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norm=
al 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>At the end of =
the day, there=E2=80=99s likely going to be a drawn-out process of coalitio=
n talks before we know who can form the next government =E2=80=94 if anyone=
=2E</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Do issues around Gaza and th=
e West Bank feature at all in the election=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:no=
rmal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Not as much =
as people outside of Israel might imagine. Nobody=E2=80=99s clamoring for I=
srael to withdraw from Gaza at this point. And even on the subject of the W=
est Bank, a lot of the opposition parties are treading very carefully. They=
 say they would rein in the extremists attacking Palestinians. But mostly t=
hey=E2=80=99re trying to avoid the issue for fear of alienating the soft-ri=
ght voters they=E2=80=99re trying to win over. The only side mentioning the=
 prospect of a Palestinian state is the pro-Netanyahu camp, which is saying=
 that will be the outcome if it loses.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17=
px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>As for the internat=
ional criticism, that=E2=80=99s barely featuring at all.</span></p><table b=
